Short-Term Ferry Ticket Demand Forecasting & Predictive Decision Support System

1. Abstract
This research investigates the application of machine learning and time-series forecasting to predict short-term ferry ticket demand for Toronto Island Park.
Ferry operations face recurring challenges in managing sudden surges in passenger traffic, particularly during weekends, holidays, and peak tourist seasons.
Traditional reactive approaches often result in congestion, delays, and safety risks. By leveraging historical ticket sales and redemption data, this project
develops predictive models capable of forecasting demand in upcoming 15-minute to 2-hour windows. The study compares baseline statistical methods with
advanced machine learning and time-series models, evaluates their accuracy using multiple metrics, and integrates results into a Streamlit dashboard 
for real-time decision support. The findings demonstrate how predictive intelligence can enhance operational efficiency, improve passenger experience, 
and support proactive planning for government stakeholders responsible for public transport services.

2. Introduction
Toronto Island Park is a major recreational destination, attracting thousands of visitors daily during peak seasons. Ferry services act as the primary mode of
transport, making efficient scheduling and crowd management critical to public safety and service quality. Currently, ferry operations rely on reactive
responses to demand fluctuations, which often leads to overcrowding, long wait times, and strained staff resources. The goal of this research is to transition 
operations from reactive analytics to predictive intelligence. By forecasting ticket demand in short-term intervals, operators can anticipate congestion before
it occurs, allocate staff more effectively, and adjust ferry schedules proactively. This approach aligns with modern smart city initiatives, where data-driven
decision-making enhances public service delivery. The study emphasizes the importance of integrating machine learning into transportation planning,
demonstrating how predictive models can directly support operational resilience and improve passenger satisfaction.
